                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The art is nice, but the game suffers from being both too short & too long. There are 22 endings, but getting to those endings doesn't take a huge amount of time, since there's an awful lot of repeated content once you've unlocked a couple.\nThe endings themselves are differentiated with some flavour text and the occasional new CG. The game would be a lot more enjoyable if there were far fewer routes, but with more unique content between them, especially for the price.\n\nThere's quite a lot of dissonance between how characters are presented visually, and how they feel. You make choices that a character seems to like/enjoy, and yet that causes the game to end with them hating you. Lots of choices should have no bearing on the story, yet how you act in a minor scene with a minor character is the difference between a happy ever after & getting jailed for life. There's no logic to predict how a choice affects the ending - follow a guide if you want to see all the endings, elsewise you'll probably waste a lot of time.\n\nThe MC is also presented oddly - at the start of the game, it seemed to suggest that you're quite young & inexperienced, a fairly morally blank person. However, it's clear after a few hours that you are actually playing a deviant who is really not a good guy.\n\nThere's also a lot of timeskipping, and (especially in the endings) the MC's relationships with the other characters are told, rather than shown. You might marry a character that you've had only half-a-dozen interactions with onscreen, or even fewer.\n\nIn summary, don't play this if you actually care about meaningful choices & gameplay. Get it on sale (at least 20% discount, IMO) for some clean artwork.",
